				<h1>Which questionnaire should I take?</h1>
				<p class="subtitle">A guide to post-viral self-assessment tools</p>
			</div>

			<div class="prose">
				<p>If you have arrived here from a search or from elsewhere on the site, you may not be sure which of the available tools is most relevant to you. This page is a brief guide to help you decide.</p>

				<h2>If your main concern is autonomic symptoms</h2>
				<p>If your main concern is autonomic symptoms (dizziness on standing, heart racing, temperature regulation problems, or gut dysfunction), start with the COMPASS-31. It is the most comprehensive autonomic symptom questionnaire available and gives you a structured, scored summary of symptom burden across six domains. It is particularly likely to be recognised by a specialist if you bring results to an appointment. It takes about 10 minutes.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/compass-31/">COMPASS-31 questionnaire</a></p>

				<h2>If breathlessness is a significant symptom</h2>
				<p>The Nijmegen questionnaire screens for breathing pattern disorder and hyperventilation syndrome, a common and often underdiagnosed problem in post-viral conditions that can contribute to breathlessness, dizziness, brain fog, and chest tightness. It takes about 2 minutes. The <a href="/tools/self-evaluation-of-breathing-questionnaire/">Self-Evaluation of Breathing Questionnaire (SEBQ)</a> asks only about breathing sensations and picks up the restricted, effortful, upper-chest patterns the Nijmegen misses; respiratory physiotherapists often use both. It takes about 5 minutes.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/nijmegen/">Nijmegen questionnaire</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/self-evaluation-of-breathing-questionnaire/">SEBQ</a></p>

				<h2>If fatigue is dominating your life</h2>
				<p>Two well-established scales measure different things. The <a href="/tools/chalder-fatigue-scale/">Chalder Fatigue Scale</a> asks how much your physical and mental fatigue have changed compared with when you were last well; it is the standard in UK ME/CFS and long COVID services and trials, so it is the score a UK clinic is most likely to recognise. The <a href="/tools/fss/">Fatigue Severity Scale (FSS)</a> measures how much fatigue interferes with daily functioning and is more common in neurology and US research. Each takes about 2 minutes.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/chalder-fatigue-scale/">Chalder Fatigue Scale (UK standard)</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/fss/">Fatigue Severity Scale</a></p>

				<h2>If brain fog is your main complaint</h2>
				<p>The <a href="/tools/cognitive-failures-questionnaire/">Cognitive Failures Questionnaire</a> counts how often everyday lapses of memory, attention, and action have happened over the past six months. It is the most widely used self-report measure of cognitive slips and is increasingly used in long COVID research to quantify brain fog. It takes about 5 minutes and gives a total out of 100 with reference values.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/cognitive-failures-questionnaire/">Cognitive Failures Questionnaire</a></p>

				<h2>If you suspect ME/CFS, or your symptoms worsen after activity</h2>
				<p>The feature that most distinguishes ME/CFS from ordinary fatigue is post-exertional malaise: symptoms flaring in the hours or days after physical or mental effort. If that pattern sounds familiar, start with the <a href="/tools/dsq-brief/">DSQ-Brief</a>, a 4-question screen (fatigue, tiredness after minimal exercise, unrefreshing sleep and memory problems) that takes under two minutes. To look at the post-activity pattern in detail (triggers and recovery time), the <a href="/tools/dsq-pem/">DSQ-PEM</a> takes about three minutes. For a thorough record to take to an appointment, the full <a href="/tools/dsq-2/">DSQ-2</a> asks all 79 of its symptom questions and checks your answers against the four major ME/CFS case definitions using DePaul's published rules. It is long, about 100 answers, and your progress is saved as you go.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/dsq-brief/">DSQ-Brief (quick screen)</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/dsq-pem/">DSQ-PEM (post-exertional malaise)</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/dsq-2/">DSQ-2 (full questionnaire)</a></p>

				<h2>If you want to check for excessive daytime sleepiness</h2>
				<p>The Epworth Sleepiness Scale measures your tendency to doze in everyday situations. Fatigue and sleepiness are not the same thing; a low ESS score alongside severe fatigue can be informative in itself. It takes about 2 minutes.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/epworth/">Epworth Sleepiness Scale</a></p>

				<h2>If you want to measure your functional capacity</h2>
				<p>Three tools at three levels of detail. The <a href="/tools/bell-scale/">Bell CFIDS Disability Scale</a> gives you a single score from 0 to 100 in under a minute, so it suits regular tracking. The <a href="/tools/pcfs/">Post-COVID-19 Functional Status scale</a> grades the impact on your daily life from 0 to 4 via four quick questions; it is the standard measure in long COVID research. The <a href="/tools/funcap/">FUNCAP questionnaire</a>, developed by and for people with ME/CFS, profiles what 27 specific activities cost you. It is the most detailed of the three and the most useful for disability paperwork.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/bell-scale/">Bell scale (quickest)</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/pcfs/">PCFS (long COVID standard)</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/funcap/">FUNCAP (most detailed)</a></p>

				<h2>If you suspect POTS or orthostatic intolerance</h2>
				<p>The NASA lean test guide walks you through an at-home standing test with a timer and heart rate logging. It is a practical way to gather objective data while waiting for formal autonomic testing, and the results can be useful to bring to appointments. Three symptom scores cover different time frames. The <a href="/tools/vanderbilt-orthostatic-symptom-score/">Vanderbilt Orthostatic Symptom Score (VOSS)</a> is rated right now while standing, so it belongs at the end of a lean test. The <a href="/tools/malmo-pots-score/">Malmö POTS Symptom Score (MAPS)</a> covers the past week and is the first score designed specifically for POTS. The <a href="/tools/orthostatic-grading-scale/">Orthostatic Grading Scale (OGS)</a> grades your usual pattern in five questions and suits a monthly baseline.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/nasa-lean-test/">NASA lean test guide</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/vanderbilt-orthostatic-symptom-score/">VOSS (while standing)</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/malmo-pots-score/">Malmö POTS score (past week)</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/orthostatic-grading-scale/">Orthostatic Grading Scale (usual pattern)</a></p>
				<p>For a step-by-step walkthrough of using these together, see <a href="/testing/do-i-have-pots/">Do I have POTS?</a></p>

				<h2>If you or a clinician have raised hypermobility</h2>
				<p>Start with the <a href="/tools/beighton-score/">Beighton score</a>, the illustrated nine-point test of today's joint range. The <a href="/tools/five-part-questionnaire/">five-part questionnaire</a> takes under a minute and captures hypermobility you used to have, which matters if you are older or injured. If hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is the question, the <a href="/tools/heds-criteria/">hEDS criteria checker</a> walks through all three of the 2017 criteria and shows which items can only be settled by a doctor.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/beighton-score/">Beighton score</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/five-part-questionnaire/">Five-part questionnaire</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/heds-criteria/">hEDS criteria checker</a></p>

				<h2>If you want the big picture: quality of life and well-being</h2>
				<p>The <a href="/tools/rand-36/">RAND-36</a> (the same 36 items as the SF-36) measures health-related quality of life across eight scales. It is used in research across almost every condition, so your profile can be compared with published ME/CFS and long COVID cohorts. It takes 5–10 minutes and suits occasional use, every few months. The <a href="/tools/who-5/">WHO-5 Well-Being Index</a> is the opposite trade-off: five questions, under a minute, sensitive to change, and well suited to monthly tracking.</p>
				<p>Two shorter scales look at the same picture from different angles. The <a href="/tools/satisfaction-with-life-scale/">Satisfaction with Life Scale</a> asks for a considered judgement of your life as a whole, with no time frame, so it is more stable than the WHO-5 and shows adjustment over months. The <a href="/tools/brief-resilience-scale/">Brief Resilience Scale</a> measures how quickly you recover from stress, which a dysregulated system does slowly; it is a capacity measure rather than a symptom measure, and it moves when load and support change.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/rand-36/">RAND-36 health survey</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/who-5/">WHO-5 Well-Being Index</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/satisfaction-with-life-scale/">Satisfaction with Life Scale</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/brief-resilience-scale/">Brief Resilience Scale</a></p>

				<h2>If you want a record of your activity level</h2>
				<p>Neither of these is a target, and in a condition with post-exertional malaise a "low" result is often the right one. They are for recording what you actually did, so that changes in capacity are visible and a clinician sees your real activity rather than a guess. The <a href="/tools/ipaq-short-form/">IPAQ short form</a> covers the last 7 days in detail, including walking and sitting time, and gives MET-minutes per week. The <a href="/tools/godin-shephard-leisure-time-exercise-questionnaire/">Godin-Shephard questionnaire</a> takes a minute and counts only leisure-time exercise sessions, which makes it quick to repeat weekly.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/ipaq-short-form/">IPAQ short form (detailed, includes sitting)</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="/tools/godin-shephard-leisure-time-exercise-questionnaire/">Godin-Shephard (one minute)</a></p>

				<h2>If you want an honest look at alcohol</h2>
				<p>Alcohol worsens sleep, lowers overnight HRV and pulls fluid, which works against everything a post-viral or orthostatic condition needs, and many people find their tolerance collapses. Others lean on it to sleep or cope. The <a href="/tools/audit/">AUDIT</a> is the WHO's ten-question screen used across the NHS; it gives a score out of 40 with the risk zones and shows whether consumption, dependence or harm is driving it. Two minutes, and worth doing before starting any new medication.</p>
				<p>→ <a href="/tools/audit/">AUDIT alcohol screening test</a></p>

				<h2>Using more than one</h2>
				<p>You can complete more than one. For someone with a post-viral condition, a reasonable starting set might be the COMPASS-31 (autonomic symptoms), the FSS (fatigue impact), and the NASA lean test (orthostatic heart rate). Together, these give a fairly rounded picture of the areas most commonly affected.</p>
